@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-applymergepatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386) applyMergePatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Some clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format (RFC 7386) rather than
+JSON Patch (RFC 6902). Export `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts` that applies a
+merge patch to a target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+
+## What I did
+
+- Test-first, since this is new behaviour: added 17 tests to `src/index.test.ts` covering every
+  RFC 7386 Appendix A example (replacing/adding a member, `null` removing a member, scalar/array
+  values replacing each other wholesale, recursive merging of nested objects, arrays replaced
+  wholesale rather than merged element-wise, a non-object patch — `null`, a scalar, or an array —
+  replacing the target entirely, and building up nested objects from an empty target while pruning
+  a nested `null`), plus two tests for non-mutation of the target (including of a nested object the
+  patch doesn't touch). Confirmed all 17 failed against the unmodified code (import error, since
+  `applyMergePatch` did not exist yet).
+- Implemented `applyMergePatch` in `src/index.ts` directly from the RFC 7386 §2 pseudocode: if
+  `patch` is not a JSON object, it replaces the target outright; otherwise each member of `patch`
+  is merged into a shallow copy of `target` (or into `{}` if `target` isn't an object), recursing
+  into `applyMergePatch` for non-null values and deleting the member when the patch value is
+  `null`. Reused the existing `isRecord` helper already shared by `applyPatch`.
+- Kept the project's immutability invariant (recorded in dl-002 for `applyPatch`, and flagged there
+  as something a future JSON Merge Patch feature should keep): `target` is never mutated, since
+  every level of the recursion builds a new object via `{ ...target }`/`{}` rather than writing
+  into the input. A nested object the patch doesn't touch is carried over by reference (same as
+  `applyPatch` does for untouched parts of a document), which is safe precisely because nothing
+  ever mutates in place.
+- Ran `node --test`: 62/62 passing (45 pre-existing + 17 new).
+- Type-checked `src/index.ts` in isolation against the project's strict `tsconfig.json`, using a
+  temporary, uncommitted `typescript` install (same approach as dl-001/task-003): clean. Did not
+  add `typescript`, `@types/node`, or a typecheck script to the project. No linter is configured in
+  this project (no ESLint config present), so there was nothing to run there.
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `src/index.ts`: added and exported `applyMergePatch`.
+- `src/index.test.ts`: added RFC 7386 Appendix A example tests and two non-mutation tests;
+  imports `applyMergePatch`.
